WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.977
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.977 --> 00:00:10.163
When I was a little kid I had a lot
of nightmares, which was weird,

00:00:10.163 --> 00:00:16.505
because I had a very happy and healthy and
safe childhood, and I had a loving family.

00:00:16.505 --> 00:00:17.802
So it was just weird.

00:00:17.802 --> 00:00:20.749
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Being a kid is weird.

00:00:20.749 --> 00:00:21.838
Brains are weird.

00:00:21.838 --> 00:00:23.328
Life is weird.

00:00:23.328 --> 00:00:26.811
Whatever, I had a lot of nightmares.

00:00:26.811 --> 00:00:30.703
And some of the bad ones rank among
my most vivid childhood memories.

00:00:30.703 --> 00:00:33.002
I had dreams about evil trees.

00:00:33.002 --> 00:00:38.154
I had dreams about giant eels,
dreams about shape-shifting grandmas.

00:00:38.154 --> 00:00:41.568
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, those stay with you, over time.

00:00:41.568 --> 00:00:46.440
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Not being able to fall

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.729
asleep because you're afraid when
you're a kid is a very dark thing.

00:00:50.729 --> 00:00:53.733
But the strange thing is I really
wouldn't change any of that,

00:00:53.733 --> 00:00:55.275
I actually kind of treasure it.

00:00:55.275 --> 00:00:59.759
That's because having nightmares as
a kid and spending time with that

00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:04.330
gave me two massive blessings and
I'll share one now and one at the end.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:05.984
The first is that I became very,

00:01:05.984 --> 00:01:09.310
very good at figuring out whether
I was in a dream or in real life.

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:11.168
And knowing the difference is everything.

00:01:11.168 --> 00:01:15.238
Nightmares really tend to lose their
edge when you know they're not real.

00:01:15.238 --> 00:01:19.498
Things chase you a little bit slower and
scary things become smaller and

00:01:19.498 --> 00:01:22.065
the world just gets a little more pliable.

00:01:22.065 --> 00:01:23.780
And weird things start to happen.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:27.627
You can do strange things
when you know it's not real.

00:01:27.627 --> 00:01:33.225
There's this kind of overwhelming
euphoria and a feeling of empowerment.

00:01:33.225 --> 00:01:37.896
When I figured out that I could
fly in my dreams as a kid,

00:01:37.896 --> 00:01:40.548
it was profoundly cathartic.

00:01:40.548 --> 00:01:42.124
It was healing.

00:01:42.124 --> 00:01:45.708
because it allowed me to take something
which terrified me about my brain and

00:01:45.708 --> 00:01:48.770
being alive and
turned into something creative and joyful.

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:51.449
And I actually welcomed my nightmares,

00:01:51.449 --> 00:01:55.324
because they would help me figure
out that I was in a dream.

00:01:55.324 --> 00:01:59.864
And it wasn't until I turned 18 that I
realized this wasn't my private mental

00:01:59.864 --> 00:02:03.943
invention to beat nightmares but
a phenomenon called lucid dreaming.

00:02:03.943 --> 00:02:07.340
And lucid dreaming is where you
figure out that you're in a dream and

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:08.597
you start to control it.

00:02:08.597 --> 00:02:09.463
And you can do whatever you want.

00:02:09.463 --> 00:02:11.888
You can fly, you can breathe underwater.

00:02:11.888 --> 00:02:13.609
You can move things with your mind.

00:02:13.609 --> 00:02:15.328
You can shoot ice out of your hands.

00:02:15.328 --> 00:02:19.240
There's really no known limits to
what you can do in a lucid dream.

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:23.598
And when I got to university,
I stumbled on the work of Stephen Liberge,

00:02:23.598 --> 00:02:27.671
who's a psychophysiology PhD
from Stanford who, in the 80's,

00:02:27.671 --> 00:02:32.404
did all the foundational literature on
what lucid dreaming is and how to do it.

00:02:32.404 --> 00:02:34.991
And I just fell all the way
down this rabbit hole.

00:02:34.991 --> 00:02:36.888
I just devoured his work.

00:02:36.888 --> 00:02:41.750
And there was this online corpus of lucid
dreaming techniques put together by a very

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:44.955
eccentric community of lucid
dreamers on how to do it.

00:02:44.955 --> 00:02:48.585
And I wasn't the only one setting this,
there were other students on campus,

00:02:48.585 --> 00:02:50.627
who had mastered the art
of lucid dreaming.

00:02:50.627 --> 00:02:51.573
Flying was nothing to them.

00:02:51.573 --> 00:02:57.134
They were having out of body experiences
and composing music in their dreams and

00:02:57.134 --> 00:03:00.456
raising landscapes with
a swipe of their hand.

00:03:00.456 --> 00:03:05.318
And the craziest thing that I learned
falling down that rabbit hole was that

00:03:05.318 --> 00:03:09.791
the techniques on how to lucid dream
can be learned, practiced, and

00:03:09.791 --> 00:03:12.126
mastered by anyone willing to try.

00:03:12.126 --> 00:03:16.575
So I'm just going to walk you through
some of those techniques that I found

00:03:16.575 --> 00:03:19.251
incredibly useful over the past few years.

00:03:19.251 --> 00:03:21.642
But there's three fundamental
gates to lucid dreaming.

00:03:21.642 --> 00:03:23.810
And every night that you
don't have a lucid dream,

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:26.240
you have failed to pass through
one of these three gates.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.841
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; They are, in order,

00:03:30.841 --> 00:03:33.550
realize, did you realize
that you were in a dream?

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:35.524
That's step one.

00:03:35.524 --> 00:03:38.504
Remain, once you realized
that you were in a dream,

00:03:38.504 --> 00:03:41.167
were you able to stay
there without waking up?

00:03:41.167 --> 00:03:45.355
And then most importantly, did you
remember that that dream happened at all?

00:03:45.355 --> 00:03:48.638
So let's start by talking about the first
one, which is also my favorite.

00:03:48.638 --> 00:03:52.508
How do you have that epiphany where
you realize that you're in a dream?

00:03:52.508 --> 00:03:54.603
It's this electric moment.

00:03:54.603 --> 00:03:59.482
And the only way to create that moment
is to ask as often as possible,

00:03:59.482 --> 00:04:02.234
in reality, am I dreaming right now?

00:04:02.234 --> 00:04:05.481
And expert lucid dreamers are checking
many times a day whether they're in

00:04:05.481 --> 00:04:06.364
a dream or reality.

00:04:06.364 --> 00:04:09.542
So I'm just going to walk you through some
of those reality checks that you can use

00:04:09.542 --> 00:04:10.275
to distinguish.

00:04:10.275 --> 00:04:12.935
Number one, and
I hope people will do this with me.

00:04:12.935 --> 00:04:15.655
This is your North Star for
figuring out if your in dream,

00:04:15.655 --> 00:04:18.894
shut your nose with your hand, and
then try and breathe through it.

00:04:20.466 --> 00:04:21.716
This should not be working.

00:04:21.716 --> 00:04:26.562
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But if it is working,

00:04:26.562 --> 00:04:29.809
congratulations, you are almost
definitely dreaming right now.

00:04:29.809 --> 00:04:30.455
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:04:30.455 --> 00:04:33.324
&gt;&gt; This talk is about to get
way more interesting for you.

00:04:33.324 --> 00:04:36.088
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But for the rest of us,

00:04:36.088 --> 00:04:39.962
it's just important to remember that
breathing is always regulated by what

00:04:39.962 --> 00:04:41.364
you're doing in reality.

00:04:41.364 --> 00:04:45.013
If your sleeping body is breathing, you're
always going to be able to breathe in

00:04:45.013 --> 00:04:47.240
a dream, so
this is a great way to distinguish.

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:52.469
Another easy one is that you can take out
your phone and look at the home screen and

00:04:52.469 --> 00:04:56.629
look at the time, and put it away and
then look back at it again.

00:04:56.629 --> 00:05:00.615
If the numbers change significantly, or
if they don't look like numbers at all,

00:05:00.615 --> 00:05:02.423
you're very likely to be in a dream.

00:05:02.423 --> 00:05:07.141
That's just because brains have a hard
time holding alphanumeric symbols in

00:05:07.141 --> 00:05:10.868
place while you're in a dream,
they shift around a lot.

00:05:10.868 --> 00:05:14.645
Then there's this other one,
which is kind of frightening, honestly.

00:05:14.645 --> 00:05:16.458
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Look at your hands and

00:05:16.458 --> 00:05:18.154
just count the fingers.

00:05:18.154 --> 00:05:19.522
If you have about 10.

00:05:19.522 --> 00:05:22.209
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And they look like hands,

00:05:22.209 --> 00:05:24.112
you're probably in reality.

00:05:24.112 --> 00:05:27.148
But if not, you're almost definitely
in a dream and this is very consistent.

00:05:27.148 --> 00:05:31.656
I just have to warn you, once you realize
you've never had normal hands in your

00:05:31.656 --> 00:05:35.911
dreams your entire life and didn't notice,
it can kind of blow you away.

00:05:35.911 --> 00:05:39.184
This one's also a bit more
discreet than the nose thing, so

00:05:39.184 --> 00:05:42.592
that's a personal preference,
I'll let you decide there.

00:05:42.592 --> 00:05:45.151
And then finally, I had to include
this one because it's just too weird.

00:05:45.151 --> 00:05:48.748
Light switches don't work in dreams,
they just don't.

00:05:48.748 --> 00:05:52.975
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Brains can't handle changing the visual

00:05:52.975 --> 00:05:55.530
landscape of a space that quickly.

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:59.422
And let's just admit that plenty of light
switches don't work in real life, so

00:05:59.422 --> 00:06:01.526
you can get some false positive with this.

00:06:01.526 --> 00:06:03.994
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So just be careful with that.

00:06:03.994 --> 00:06:09.024
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Okay, so once you've achieved lucidity

00:06:09.024 --> 00:06:14.662
and you're having that moment,
how do you avoid waking up immediately?

00:06:14.662 --> 00:06:18.471
This is actually a lot harder than it
sounds, people underappreciate this.

00:06:18.471 --> 00:06:22.563
because most lucid dreams will last only
five or ten seconds, which is a bummer.

00:06:22.563 --> 00:06:26.159
Okay, so this is a bit more art
than science, but really important.

00:06:26.159 --> 00:06:30.339
You have to act super nonchalant as
soon as you realize you're in a dream.

00:06:30.339 --> 00:06:32.660
You just have to act like
you don't care at all.

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:36.044
And you're going to get a rush of
adrenaline and your heartbeat will go up.

00:06:36.044 --> 00:06:38.783
So you actually have to pretend
like someone's watching you.

00:06:38.783 --> 00:06:41.440
I have to do this every time.

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:43.992
And the point is that if you
can get through this five or

00:06:43.992 --> 00:06:45.866
ten second window of over excitement,

00:06:45.866 --> 00:06:49.128
then you're probably going to have
the whole dream, which is great.

00:06:49.128 --> 00:06:52.947
And then more concretely, you have
to focus on your body in the dream,

00:06:52.947 --> 00:06:54.454
not your body in real life.

00:06:54.454 --> 00:06:59.329
So the way I do that is, in the dream I
just rotate my hands in little circles and

00:06:59.329 --> 00:07:00.759
I just look at my body.

00:07:00.759 --> 00:07:05.039
And the point is that you can't become
aware of your sleeping body in your bed.

00:07:05.039 --> 00:07:08.107
If you do that, you're going to
get ejected out of your dream and

00:07:08.107 --> 00:07:11.178
thrown back into that body, so
that'll end it immediately.

00:07:11.178 --> 00:07:14.740
Okay, so you've had a lucid dream, how do
you actually remember that it happened?

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:19.601
And this is so important and so difficult,
because if you don't remember it,

00:07:19.601 --> 00:07:22.339
it's really as if it didn't happen at all.

00:07:22.339 --> 00:07:26.626
And this is hard,
95 to 99% of dreams are totally forgotten.

00:07:26.626 --> 00:07:29.619
And many people report
not having dreams at all,

00:07:29.619 --> 00:07:32.904
which to me is as tragic as
it is totally misinformed,

00:07:32.904 --> 00:07:37.798
because they've shown in labs over and
over that everyone dreams every night.

00:07:37.798 --> 00:07:40.073
It's just a matter of remembering it.

00:07:40.073 --> 00:07:42.108
So many people know that dream
journals really help with this.

00:07:42.108 --> 00:07:44.840
And you can train that muscle
using a dream journal.

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:47.290
But they just don't seem to work for
a lot of people.

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:50.774
And the reason is that they're missing
a key aspect, which is that the dream

00:07:50.774 --> 00:07:53.723
journal has to be at arm's length
from where you're sleeping.

00:07:53.723 --> 00:07:57.149
It has to be under your pillow or under
your mattress or on your bed side table.

00:07:57.149 --> 00:07:59.201
And the reason for
that is that standing up and

00:07:59.201 --> 00:08:01.908
moving around is what wipes
your dream memory completely.

00:08:01.908 --> 00:08:05.278
You have to practice remembering
everything from a lying down position.

00:08:05.278 --> 00:08:08.545
And if you don't have a dream journal
handy, you can just use a smartphone and

00:08:08.545 --> 00:08:09.511
take a voice memo too.

00:08:09.511 --> 00:08:12.872
Any bullets or
fragments you remember help with this, and

00:08:12.872 --> 00:08:15.348
it is a muscle that you
can train over time.

00:08:15.348 --> 00:08:18.530
And then much more bizarrely,
and this has been shown to

00:08:18.530 --> 00:08:23.133
work by the National Sleep Foundation in
empirical studies, just say out loud,

00:08:23.133 --> 00:08:26.058
I want to remember my dreams,
before you go to bed.

00:08:26.058 --> 00:08:27.286
This actually works.

00:08:27.286 --> 00:08:28.541
And the basis for

00:08:28.541 --> 00:08:33.299
this is in the psychology dark
magic of cognitive dissonance.

00:08:33.299 --> 00:08:35.399
And we definitely don't have
time to talk about that.

00:08:35.399 --> 00:08:39.149
But suffice to say, this is easy and
we should all be doing it.

00:08:39.149 --> 00:08:41.380
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Okay, so

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:43.546
those are some of
the important techniques.

00:08:43.546 --> 00:08:44.371
But why does this matter?

00:08:44.371 --> 00:08:48.198
Why am I even talking about this?

00:08:48.198 --> 00:08:52.508
Flying is cool, we like flying.

00:08:52.508 --> 00:08:55.408
It's a proven nightmare therapy,
we like that.

00:08:55.408 --> 00:08:57.825
Nightmares are bad.

00:08:57.825 --> 00:09:02.281
But I think it's worth admitting that no
one really knows why this is valuable or

00:09:02.281 --> 00:09:03.753
even why it's possible.

00:09:03.753 --> 00:09:08.063
There's no scientific consensus
on why humans need to sleep,

00:09:08.063 --> 00:09:10.095
much less why they dream, and

00:09:10.095 --> 00:09:15.565
lucid dreaming's thought to be more a bug
than a feature of human development.

00:09:15.565 --> 00:09:17.700
So why is this worth our time and
attention?

00:09:19.288 --> 00:09:24.276
The second blessing that having nightmares
as a kid gave me was a truth that I've

00:09:24.276 --> 00:09:26.510
always taken to be self-evident,

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:32.084
which is that having a healthy
relationship with your mind is everything.

00:09:32.084 --> 00:09:32.893
That's everything.

00:09:32.893 --> 00:09:33.739
That's what life is.

00:09:33.739 --> 00:09:37.705
Life is whatever happens in
your brain before you die.

00:09:37.705 --> 00:09:42.956
And on one hand, that's terrifying,
because brains aren't always good,

00:09:42.956 --> 00:09:47.981
they'll sometimes do stuff like give
kids nightmares, which is weird.

00:09:47.981 --> 00:09:51.939
And living life in the cage of
your mind is kind of a bummer.

00:09:51.939 --> 00:09:55.224
But over time,
in the process of lucid dreaming,

00:09:55.224 --> 00:09:59.605
which I can only describe as spiritual,
I've come to realize that

00:09:59.605 --> 00:10:04.066
turning your mind into a creative
space where you can experiment and

00:10:04.066 --> 00:10:09.152
witness yourself and have that moment
of awakening where you're like,

00:10:09.152 --> 00:10:12.935
these colors, and these faces,
and these landscapes.

00:10:12.935 --> 00:10:14.106
That's all me.

00:10:14.106 --> 00:10:17.593
And just witness the mystery of your mind.

00:10:17.593 --> 00:10:20.769
That's among the greatest
joys there is in life.

00:10:20.769 --> 00:10:25.248
And people wake up from lucid dreams
excited to listen to what their mind is

00:10:25.248 --> 00:10:29.584
telling them, and then witness that
mystery throughout their life and

00:10:29.584 --> 00:10:31.554
understand their mind better.

00:10:31.554 --> 00:10:34.494
So I encourage all of you, just try it.

00:10:34.494 --> 00:10:36.267
Just play with it.

00:10:36.267 --> 00:10:41.503
It may change for you as it did for me
how you experience life in your own mind.

00:10:41.503 --> 00:10:42.310
Thank you.

00:10:42.310 --> 00:10:47.876
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:47.876 --> 00:10:49.740
[MUSIC]

